  "mechanism": {
    "learning": {
      "description": "Pretraining learns Phi and w_beta by minimizing next-token cross-entropy through the same sequential recurrence used at inference. The training mix is text plus synthetic cyclic-rule episodes: Caesar-type shifts on letters, digits, days, months and notes, with several shift rules per context and queries on undemonstrated tokens.\n\nGradients flow through:\n- the RLS statistics, where the write key is the selected row Phi[j_t], so its gradient goes to that row;\n- the readout powers (K_t^T)^{e_t}.\n\nGate (straight-through), stated per pair type.\n- Reserved pairs (x_{t-1} or x_t in {BOS, ';'}): beta_t = 0 is a constant. There is no surrogate, no gradient, and nothing extra is computed.\n- Content pairs: the forward pass uses the hard beta_t. The backward pass replaces d beta_t by d sigma_t, with sigma_t = logistic(w_beta^T[u_t; v_t]). It differentiates the gated updates u[3]-u[4], viewed as functions of beta, at the forward value of beta_t.\n- Written content pair (beta_t = 1): this is the derivative of the actual write.\n- Content pair closed by the gate (beta_t = 0): the backward pass differentiates the counterfactual gated update at beta = 0, not the skipped identity. Its derivatives are dP_t/dbeta = -P_{t-1} z' z'^T P_{t-1} and dK_t/dbeta = P_{t-1} z' (v_t^T - z'^T K_{t-1}), with z' = Phi[j'_t].\n- The counterfactual duration e'_t and key j'_t are computed in the training forward pass by the same rules u[2]-u[4]: the orbit of K_{t-1}, the test against P_{t-1}, and the snap. They use the unchanged state, are stored as integers, and serve only this derivative.\n- The forward state of a closed pair is not affected: K_t = K_{t-1}, P_t = P_{t-1}, e_t = e_{t-1} (e'_t is not copied into e_t).\n- Because beta multiplies the counterfactual write, at beta = 0 that write passes no gradient to Phi through z' or v_t. A closed pair contributes gradient only to w_beta and to the rows Phi[x_{t-1}], Phi[x_t] through sigma_t. A closed gate can therefore reopen when writing the pair would lower the loss.\n- At inference the counterfactual is never computed.\n\nThe discrete choices (e_t, j_t, e'_t, j'_t and the identification test) are piecewise constant in the parameters and are treated as constants in the backward pass. lambda, tau and M are fixed constants.\n\nNo parameter changes at inference: all in-context learning lives in K, P and e_t.",
      "equations": [
        {
          "latex": "K_t=\\Big(\\lambda I+\\sum_{s\\le t,\\ \\beta_s=1} z_s z_s^\\top\\Big)^{-1}\\sum_{s\\le t,\\ \\beta_s=1} z_s v_s^\\top,\\qquad z_s=\\Phi[j_s]\\ \\text{computed in order } s=1,2,\\dots,t",
          "name": "ridge identity for the keys produced by the recursion (not a fixed point)",
          "plain": "Run the recursion forward. For s = 1..t, e_s and j_s are computed from K_{s-1}, which is already fixed. Given that sequence of keys z_s = Phi[j_s], K_t equals solve(lam*I + sum_s outer(z_s, z_s), sum_s outer(z_s, v_s)) over the written s. This is the standard RLS = ridge identity with P_0 = I/lam and K_0 = 0, so no fixed-point iteration is involved."
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\mathcal L(\\Phi,w_\\beta)=-\\sum_{t=1}^{n-1}\\log p(x_{t+1}\\mid x_{\\le t})",
          "name": "pretraining loss",
          "plain": "loss = -sum_t log p_t[x[t+1]]"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\frac{\\partial K_t}{\\partial \\beta}\\Big|_{\\beta=0}=P_{t-1}z'(v_t^\\top-z'^\\top K_{t-1}),\\quad \\frac{\\partial P_t}{\\partial \\beta}\\Big|_{\\beta=0}=-P_{t-1}z'z'^\\top P_{t-1},\\quad z'=\\Phi[j'_t],\\quad d\\beta_t:=\\sigma_t(1-\\sigma_t)\\,d(w_\\beta^\\top[u_t;v_t])",
          "name": "straight-through gate derivative for a closed content pair (training only)",
          "plain": "dK_dbeta = outer(P_prev @ zc, v - K_prev.T @ zc); dP_dbeta = -outer(P_prev @ zc, P_prev @ zc); zc = Phi[j_cf]; grad_beta = <dL/dK_t, dK_dbeta> + <dL/dP_t, dP_dbeta>; backprop grad_beta * sigma*(1-sigma) into w_beta and into Phi[x[t-1]], Phi[x[t]]"
        }
      ],
      "few_shot_argument": "Notation.\n- x_t is a token. u = Phi[x_{t-1}] and v = Phi[x_t] are lifts.\n- z_c = (K^T)^c u are orbit points. j is the snapped key token.\n- C is the set of content tokens. Content tokens form a cycle Z_p.\n- Rule f_s maps a to a+s mod p.\n- A demonstration is the triple 'a a+s ;'. A query is 'q ;'.\n- Every pair containing ';' or BOS is skipped.\n\nQuantities (D3).\n- T_A is a d×d orthogonal matrix with Phi[a+s_A] = T_A^T Phi[a] for every a in C. For the reference Fourier lift it exists exactly: T_A = blockdiag_{m=1..8} R(-2 pi m s_A/p), with R(phi) = [[cos phi, -sin phi], [sin phi, cos phi]]. For a pretrained lift its existence is not assumed, and only the measured quantities below are used.\n- eta(K) = max over a in C of ||K^T Phi[a] - Phi[a+s_A]||. rho(K) = ||K||_2. E_c(K) = eta(K) * sum_{j=0}^{c-1} rho(K)^j.\n- delta_min = min over distinct a, b in C of ||Phi[a] - Phi[b]||.\n- gamma = min over j in C and any token b != j of (Phi[j]-Phi[b])^T Phi[j] / ||Phi[j]-Phi[b]||.\n- mu = min(delta_min/2, gamma).\n- For the reference lift (unit content rows, zero reserved rows): delta_min^2 = 2 - (1/4) sum_m cos(2 pi m/p), which is about 0.260 for p = 61 and 0.237 for p = 64. Then mu = delta_min/2, about 0.255 and 0.243.\n\nLemma 0 (orbit error, the local-to-global accumulation of 2605.00836). For any K and any a in C, ||(K^T)^c Phi[a] - Phi[a+c s_A]|| <= E_c(K). Proof: write err_c for the left side. Then err_c <= ||K^T(z_{c-1} - Phi[a+(c-1)s_A])|| + ||K^T Phi[a+(c-1)s_A] - Phi[a+c s_A]|| <= rho err_{c-1} + eta.\n\nLemma 1 (exact writes keep K on the ridge path). Suppose T_A exists and every pair written so far is an exact base pair (Phi[j], Phi[j+s_A]). Then by the ridge identity K_t = (lambda I + G_t)^{-1} G_t T_A = (I - lambda P_t) T_A. Since 0 <= lambda P_t <= I:\n- rho(K_t) <= 1;\n- eta(K_t) = lambda max_a ||P_t Phi[a]|| <= lambda ||P_t||;\n- E_M(K_t) <= M lambda ||P_t||, and ||P_t|| is non-increasing in t.\n\nGate condition (G). w_beta^T[Phi[a]; Phi[b]] >= 0 for every demonstration pair of the test. w_beta = 0 satisfies it. A skipped base pair only shrinks the spanning set. A skipped B pair leaves e_t unchanged, so that rule fails.\n\n(1) Bootstrap.\n- While u^T P u >= tau, the update sets e = 1, the snap returns x_{t-1} itself, and the write is exact.\n- In the search branch, Lemma 1 gives ||v - z_1|| = lambda ||P u|| <= sqrt(lambda u^T P u) < sqrt(lambda tau), about 0.032. So a base demonstration gets e = 1 unless some orbit point z_c with c >= 2 lies at least as close to v. Premise A1 states that this does not happen, so all base writes are exact.\n- After the 24 base keys, A2 gives smallest Gram eigenvalue sigma_min^2 >= 1e-3. Then ||P|| <= 1/(lambda + sigma_min^2) < 1000 = tau, so every later content pair goes to the search branch.\n- By Lemma 1, E_M(K) <= M lambda ||P|| <= 60 × 1e-6 × 999, about 0.06, which is below mu.\n\n(2) One-shot duration. If s_A is a unit mod p (any s_A != 0 when p is prime), there is a unique c* in {1..M} with s_B = c* s_A mod p. For a demonstration (a, a+s_B):\n- Lemma 0 gives ||v - z_{c*}|| <= E_M.\n- For c != c*, Phi[a + c s_A] != Phi[a+s_B], so ||v - z_c|| >= delta_min - E_M.\n- Hence e_t = c* whenever E_M < delta_min/2, and no ties are possible.\n\n(3) The snapped transported write is exact. By Lemma 0, ||z_{c*-1} - Phi[a+(c*-1)s_A]|| <= E_M < delta_min/2. So the nearest content row is that token: j = a+(c*-1)s_A. The written pair (Phi[j], Phi[a+c* s_A]) is then an exact base pair, often on a token never shown. By Lemma 1, K stays at (I - lambda P)T_A and E_M stays below M lambda ||P_{t0}|| (t0 = end of the base block) after any number of new-rule writes. The state holds no per-rule slot and accumulates no error. Without the snap, the approximate key would add up to ||P_t|| (eta + rho E_M) to eta per write. With ||P|| near 1 and c up to 60 this is a possible amplification of about 60 per write, which no bound controls, and this is why the key is snapped.\n\n(4) Correct prediction on unseen tokens. The pairs around the query are skipped, so e_t = c* persists. The readout y = (K^T)^{c*} Phi[q] satisfies ||y - Phi[q+s_B]|| <= E_M. For any token b != q+s_B, the logit difference is at least ||Phi[q+s_B] - Phi[b]|| (gamma - E_M) > 0, so the argmax over all V tokens, reserved ones included, is correct when E_M < gamma. A later single cue of A has c* = 1, so recall after interruption is immediate.\n\nSo about d demonstrations identify the flow once. After that, 1 demonstration identifies each further rule in the cyclic semigroup {T_A^c : c <= M}. The mechanism is the semigroup law plus exact transported writes, not re-fitting.\n\nLimits.\n- Pairs of another rule that arrive before the base keys span R^d are written with e = 1 as wrong base pairs and corrupt K. Lemma 1 then fails.\n- A rule outside the semigroup gets the nearest power (ties to the smallest c), predicts wrongly, and its snapped write is a wrong base pair. Examples are a reflection a -> -a, or an odd shift when p = 64 and s_A is even.\n- E_M grows linearly in M, so M lambda ||P_{t0}|| must stay below mu.\n- The core holds one generator."
    },
