  "non_reduction_argument": {
    "broken_invariant": "The SUGS mapping relies on two facts.\n- (a) The components are independent conjugate linear-Gaussian regressions. Each has sufficient statistics sum_{i assigned to j} (u_i u_i^T, u_i v_i^T) over the raw regressor/target pairs assigned to it. The bank is therefore a sum of per-pair writes, with u_i a function of token x_{i-1} only.\n- (b) A pair assigned to component j changes only component j's predictive.\n\nThis card breaks both in updates u[1] to u[4].\n- There is one operator K. The candidate components are its powers K^c, whose predictive (K^T)^c u is a degree-c polynomial in the state, so no per-component conjugate posterior exists.\n- The write uses the key Phi[j_t], with j_t the content token nearest to (K_{t-1}^T)^{e_t-1} u_t. The statistics are therefore sum_s (z_s z_s^T, z_s v_s^T) over key tokens inferred from the whole prefix, not given by the input token.\n- A duration-3 pair changes the predictions for every duration at once.\n\nThe toy example shows the break. The B pair (1,4) is written with key Phi[3], a token absent from the pair, and the one-shot query is answered correctly (0) where the SUGS fresh component answers 4.",
    "known_family": "other_known: sequential greedy (SUGS) Dirichlet-process / CRP mixture of conjugate Bayesian linear regressions (Mixture-of-Memories MesaNet with a likelihood router)",
    "why_unrepairable": "Repair 1 (strongest): SUGS with one component per duration c, i.e. M independent conjugate regressions routed by predictive likelihood. Each component would be fitted only from pairs routed to it, so a rule seen once has a one-pair posterior. That is exactly the fresh_mode_pred = 4 baseline in the toy. To match the card, the posterior mean of component c would have to equal the c-th power of component 1's mean, and every pair routed to c would have to update component 1 at an inferred key token. No conjugate mixture has that nonlinear tie.\n\nRepair 2: MesaNet or linear attention with a learned key map. The key token j_t depends on the operator identified so far and on a discrete test against it, so no feature map of the input tokens produces it. In the toy, input token 1 would get key Phi[1] if the base were unidentified or if e = 1, and it gets key Phi[3] here.\n\nRepair 3: Mixture-of-Memories with a likelihood router, or a lifted state enumerating durations. Both store separate per-duration statistics, so neither can transfer one demonstration of rule A^3 into the operator of A.\n\nRepair 4: TTT on the loss ||(K^T)^e u - v||^2. This needs e as an input, and it takes a gradient step through the power instead of an exact RLS write of the snapped one-step pair, so it gives a different K after the same pair. A critic can check this on the toy's third write.\n\nRepair 5: a discrete-logarithm or subgroup routine over given group labels. No group or labels are given. The group is the semigroup of an operator identified in context on a learned lift, and identification and exponent inference are coupled through the transported keys.\n\nThe observable differences are the claims themselves:\n- one-shot new rules without slots, where MesaNet and SUGS fail;\n- the odd/even split on a cycle of 64, which no mixture predicts;\n- order dependence, where MesaNet is order-invariant."
