  "summary": "Notation: x_t is always a token. u_t = Phi[x_{t-1}] and v_t = Phi[x_t] are token lifts. z_{t,c} are orbit points. j_t is the snapped key token.\n\nPretraining learns a token lift Phi, tied as the decoder, and a hard pair gate w_beta. At inference the whole in-context state is one operator K (d×d), its inverse Gram matrix P, and an integer duration e_t.\n\nPairs that contain a reserved token (BOS or ';') are always skipped. Content pairs with w_beta^T[u_t; v_t] < 0 are also skipped. For each written adjacent pair (x_{t-1}, x_t), the model forms the orbit z_{t,c} = (K_{t-1}^T)^c u_t for c = 0..M.\n- If u_t lies in directions the operator has not identified yet (u_t^T P_{t-1} u_t >= tau), the duration is 1.\n- Otherwise the duration e_t is the power c in {1..M} whose orbit point lands closest to v_t.\n\nThe pair is then written into K by recursive least squares. The key is Phi[j_t], the lift of the content token nearest to the transported orbit point z_{t,e_t-1}, not the raw lift u_t. A demonstration of rule B = A^3 therefore becomes an exact one-step demonstration of A at a token that may never have appeared. Because every such write is an exact base pair, the operator stays on the ridge path of the base flow, and its error does not grow with the number of rules.\n\nPrediction applies the operator for the most recently inferred duration: y_t = (K_t^T)^{e_t} Phi[x_t].\n\nConsequences:\n- After about d demonstrations of one rule identify the flow, each further rule in the cyclic semigroup it generates (every shift on a prime cycle) is used correctly after one demonstration.\n- The number of rules is not capped by slots.\n- Group-theoretic predictions follow. For example, on a cycle of 64 an even base shift cannot express an odd new shift.\n\nThe write key depends on the prefix through powers of the current operator and a nearest-token test, and the 'components' (the powers K^c) are tied rather than independent. So the state is neither a sum of per-token writes nor a mixture of independent conjugate regressions. Cost is O(M d^2 + V d) per token.",
